 ],
 "literal": "Who made loose the bangles on my long, rounded shoulders / the lord of the land of hills, who has become cruel, / when he comes, without my sweet face changing, / with divine chastity, welcoming him, / 'You are indeed foolish,' thus questioning me, / do not grieve, may you live, my friend! Noble ones / even before praise, they feel shy; / will they commit a blameworthy act, when we consider?",
 "translation": "My friend, may you live long! Do not be angry with me. You question me, saying, 'You are surely foolish to welcome him with a pleasant, unchanging face, with divine devotion, when he—the lord of the hilly land who has become cruel—comes, having caused the bangles to slip from my long, rounded shoulders.' But consider this: noble people feel shy even before they are praised; would they ever commit a blameworthy act when the time comes?",
 "modern": "என் நீண்ட திரண்ட தோள்களின் வளையல்களை நெகிழச் செய்த கொடியவனாகிய குன்றுகளையுடைய நாடன் நம்மிடம் வரும்போது, என்னுடைய இனிய முகம் வேறுபடாமல், தெய்வத்தன்மை பொருந்திய கற்போடு அவனை எதிர்கொண்டு உபசரித்ததைக் கண்டு, 'நீ மிகவும் பேதை' என்று என்னை வினவி வருந்தாதே தோழி! சான்றோர்கள் தங்களைப் புகழ்ந்து பேசுவதற்கு முன்னரே நாணமடைவார்கள்; அப்படிப்பட்டவர்கள் ஆராய்ந்து பார்க்கும்போது பழி தரும் செயலைச் செய்வார்களா?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend who has reproached her for welcoming the returning hero with a warm, unchanging face. The friend feels the heroine is too naive and forgiving, especially since the hero's long absence caused her so much distress that her bangles slipped from her shoulders. The heroine defends her behavior, explaining that her welcoming attitude is a reflection of her steadfast love and trust. She uses a general truth about noble people, stating that those of high character feel embarrassed even when praised. Therefore, she argues, a noble person like the hero would never deliberately commit an act that brings blame or dishonor. This poem belongs to the Kuriñci landscape, where the hero's association with the enduring hills reflects the high character and nobility attributed to him by the heroine.",
